## 6. Port Selection

Nmap supports several options for choosing which ports to scan.

| Option | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-p22,80,443` | Scans ports 22, 80, and 443. |
| `-p1-1023` | Scans ports 1 through 1023, inclusive. |
| `-p20-25` | Scans ports 20 through 25, inclusive. |
| `-p-` | Scans all TCP or UDP port numbers from 1 through 65535 for the selected scan type. |
| `-F` | Scans 100 commonly used ports from Nmap's port-frequency database. |
| `--top-ports 10` | Scans the ten most common ports for the relevant protocol. |

### Examples

Scan selected ports:

```bash
nmap -p22,80,443 TARGET_IP
```

Scan ports 1 through 1023:

```bash
nmap -p1-1023 TARGET_IP
```

Scan all TCP ports:

```bash
nmap -p- TARGET_IP
```

Scan the ten most common UDP ports:

```bash
sudo nmap -sU --top-ports 10 TARGET_IP
```

**Important:** `-p-` does not automatically scan every TCP and UDP port together. It applies to the selected scan type. TCP and UDP scans can be requested together when required.

